from author_proof_obligations import add
add('P187-0002','localization and sheafification identification omitted','§4.2, equation (4.2.3): locality of sheaf Ext','Sheaf Ext commutes with restriction to an open subset and is the sheafification of local Ext.','Restrict an injective resolution to the open subset and use the equality of internal Hom under open restriction. Exact sheafification identifies the sheaf cohomology of that Hom complex with sheafification of its presheaf cohomology.','sheaf Ext|open restriction|sheafification')
add('P188-0001','bifunctor exactness verification omitted','§4.2: cohomological behavior in the first argument','For an injective second argument, both global Hom and internal Hom are exact contravariantly in the first argument.','Global exactness is injectivity. On every open subset restriction of the injective sheaf remains injective, so the presheaf Hom sequences are exact there; this gives exactness of internal Hom and the boundary functor through the resolution.','Ext bifunctor|internal Hom|injective')
add('P189-0001','universal-functor comparison compressed','Theorem 4.2.2: stalk comparison for Ext','Under the stated finiteness and stalk-injectivity assumptions, the natural map from sheaf-Ext stalks to module Ext is an isomorphism.','Check the degree-zero Hom comparison by finite presentation and efface the target positive degrees using the injective-stalk result. The universal delta-functor uniqueness then gives all degrees and compatibility with connecting maps.','stalk Ext|finite presentation|injective stalk')
add('P189-0002','local-free vanishing asserted','Proposition 4.2.3: locally free first argument','For a locally finite free first argument, higher internal Ext vanishes and the stalk Ext comparison is trivial in positive degrees.','On a trivializing open set internal Hom is a finite direct sum of the identity functor, hence exact; at a stalk the first argument is finite free and therefore projective. Locality glues the vanishing.','locally free|sheaf Ext|projective module')
add('P189-0003','spectral-sequence collapse and direct derivation','Corollary to Proposition 4.2.3: cohomology as Ext','For a locally finite free first argument, global Ext is cohomology of internal Hom; in particular Ext^p(O,B)=H^p(X,B).','Collapse the local-to-global Ext spectral sequence using the local Ext vanishing. For A=O identify global Hom with sections and use that injective O-modules are section-acyclic as established earlier. The module category may change while its derived section groups agree with ordinary sheaf cohomology.','local to global Ext|cohomology as Ext')
add('P190-0001','corollary delegated to general resolving theorem','Proposition 4.2.4, Corollary 1: computing Ext','The resolution described in Proposition 4.2.4 computes the corresponding derived Hom functor.','Verify all hypotheses of Proposition 2.5.1 for the resolving complex, especially exactness and vanishing on injective second arguments, then identify the degree-zero augmentation.','Ext resolution|resolving functor')
add('P190-0002','recursive compatibility argument omitted','§4.2, equations (4.2.11)–(4.2.12): Hom and the canonical resolution','For the finite-presentation objects used here, internal Hom commutes with the Godement-type canonical resolution.','The degree-zero map uses the finite-presentation stalk-Hom comparison. At every recursion stage the stalk inclusion into the product envelope has the evaluation retraction, so the cokernel sequence is split on stalks. Apply Hom of the finitely presented stalk and the comparison again to justify passing to the next quotient; a general assertion that Hom preserves arbitrary cokernels would be false.','Godement resolution|finite presentation|internal Hom')
add('P191-0001','finite-rank comparison and Kunneth reduction','Proposition 4.3.2: constant coefficients and two filtrations','A finite-rank free resolution identifies the relevant double-complex filtration with local-to-global Ext; the remaining calculation is a Kunneth problem.','Use finite rank to identify sheaf Hom of each constant free term with a finite sum, and apply the preceding resolving comparison. When finite rank is absent, keep the other spectral sequence but do not assert the same sheaf-level identification.','constant sheaf|Kunneth|finite rank resolution')
add('P192-0001','semisimple collapse stated','Proposition 4.3.2, Corollary 1: semisimple quotient','If the coefficient action factors through a semisimple quotient, the displayed double-complex cohomology has the canonical graded-Hom description and the two spectral sequences degenerate.','Factor the Hom complex through K tensor_O L, split cycles and boundaries over the semisimple ring, and identify the natural map on cohomology with the graded Hom of homology. Check the total degree and finite diagonals of the first-quadrant double complex.','semisimple|Kunneth|Tor|Ext')
add('P192-0002','Kunneth computation referred to standard theory','§4.3, equations (4.3.4)–(4.3.5)','The Hom-over-K reformulation gives the Tor and sheaf-cohomology formula.','Use the tensor-Hom adjunction, exactness of Hom_K in both variables, and split-complex decompositions to show the cohomology map is an isomorphism. The isomorphism on cohomology is natural although chosen splittings used to prove it need not be.','Kunneth|semisimple|tensor Hom')
add('P193-0001','filtered spectral-sequence passage asserted','Proposition 4.3.2, Remark 2: stalk spectral sequences','Taking filtered neighborhood colimits yields spectral sequences abutting to stalks of sheaf Ext.','Take the filtered colimit page by page, using exactness of filtered colimits of groups, and compare the finite filtrations in each total degree. Do not move the colimit inside an Ext or Hom term before verifying a separate commutation hypothesis.','stalk spectral sequence|filtered colimit|Ext')
add('P193-0002','commutation criteria and counterexample existence asserted','Proposition 4.3.2, Remark 2: limits through Ext','The stalk formula follows when the relevant Ext terms commute with the neighborhood colimit; finite noetherian Tor modules give one sufficient case, while arbitrary infinite-rank modules can fail.','For the noetherian case resolve each finite Tor module by finite free modules and commute the resulting Hom complexes with filtered colimits. For the HLC example supply the required local cohomological constancy in the convention used, rather than assuming every locally zero presheaf commutes with arbitrary Hom. The countable free-group/convergent-sequence example in the check events gives an explicit failure outside the finiteness hypotheses.','stalk Ext|HLC|noetherian Tor|infinite product','p193:4.3.2-remark2-noetherian|p193:stalk-failure-example')
add('P193-0003','change of language verification','§4.4: group actions as modules over a group algebra','A sheaf of k-modules with a G-action is a module over the constant group algebra when G acts trivially on the base and k.','Extend the action k-linearly, and check the group law is exactly the module-associativity relation. The augmentation ideal annihilates the sheaf precisely when every group element acts as the identity.','group algebra|G-sheaf|augmentation ideal')
add('P194-0001','spectral-sequence specialization and collapse','Proposition 4.4.1, equation (4.4.1)','The two spectral sequences are the specialization of the Ext constructions to group cohomology; trivial action over a field gives the stated collapse.','Substitute O=k[G] and the augmentation module into the previous double complex, check the group-degree and space-degree conventions, and use the semisimple coefficient calculation only with the stated trivial-action and field hypotheses.','equivariant cohomology|group cohomology|spectral sequence')
add('P194-0002','universal-coefficient specialization','Proposition 4.4.1, equation (4.4.3)','For trivial group action the indicated exact sequence is the preceding hereditary-ring universal-coefficient sequence.','Apply the degree-one Ext/Tor decomposition over Z with its two nonzero columns, preserving the n and n−1 degrees and distinguishing a canonical exact sequence from a chosen splitting.','universal coefficient|group homology|trivial action')
add('P195-0001','HLC constant-coefficient application','Proposition 4.4.1, Remark 2','Under the stated HLC and field hypotheses, the equivariant cohomology is expressed through bilinear maps from group and space homology.','Compare constant-sheaf cohomology to the appropriate topological cohomology under the precise HLC assumptions, apply the field universal-coefficient formula, and take the total graded sum. This does not identify the internal group-cohomology sheaf with a constant sheaf without the separate stalk hypotheses.','HLC|constant coefficients|group homology|Kunneth')
add('P195-0002','quotient-map openness verification','§5.1: orbit space','The quotient map of a space by a group of homeomorphisms is open.','For an open U, the inverse image of its quotient image is the union of all translates gU, which is open; the quotient topology therefore makes the image open.','orbit space|open quotient|group action')
add('P195-0003','equivariant-category properties asserted broadly','§5.1: categories of equivariant sheaves','The basic sheaf constructions carry compatible group actions, giving the category properties used below.','Construct the actions on kernels, cokernels, limits and colimits by functoriality and verify equivariance of their universal maps. Interpret the broad phrase same properties through these specific constructions; it does not remove the need to prove enough injectives or the generator separately.','equivariant sheaf|kernel cokernel|group action')
add('P196-0001','semilinear action interpretation omitted','§5.1: equivariant module sheaves','Compatible group and ring actions define an additive category; when the base action is trivial they can be encoded by a skew group-ring sheaf.','Write the relation g(a m)=g(a)g(m) and the skew multiplication (a g)(b h)=a g(b)(gh). If the ring action is also trivial this becomes the ordinary tensor product with the group algebra. Check these definitions commute with restriction.','semilinear|skew group ring|equivariant module')
add('P196-0002','generator construction sketched','Proposition 5.1.1: enough generators for equivariant sheaves','The induced sheaves L(U) represent sections over U and their sum is a generator.','Define the semilinear action by permuting the summands with the prescribed ring action, extend a chosen section equivariantly, and prove uniqueness from the identity summand. A proper equivariant subsheaf is detected by a local section outside it. The other abelian and AB5 properties are checked on underlying sheaves.','equivariant generator|induction|local section')
